  "solution": {
    "approach": "Accept a float value and print it in scientific exponential notation with 6 decimals and 2-digit exponent.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(1)",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1)",
    "code": {
      "C": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main() {\n    double d;\n    if (scanf(\"%lf\", &d) == 1) {\n        printf(\"%e\\n\", d);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n",
      "C++": "#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    double d;\n    if (cin >> d) {\n        cout << scientific << d << \"\\n\";\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n",
      "Java": "import java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        if (sc.hasNextDouble()) {\n            System.out.printf(\"%e\\n\", sc.nextDouble());\n        }\n    }\n}\n",
      "Python3": "import sys\n\ndef main():\n    s = sys.stdin.read().strip()\n    if s:\n        val = float(s)\n        print(f\"{val:e}\")\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n",
      "JavaScript": "const fs = require('fs');\n\nfunction main() {\n    const s = fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf-8').trim();\n    if (s.length > 0) {\n        const val = parseFloat(s);\n        const expStr = val.toExponential(6);\n        const [m, e] = expStr.split('e');\n        const sign = e[0];\n        const exp = e.slice(1).padStart(2, '0');\n        console.log(m + 'e' + sign + exp);\n    }\n}\n\nmain();\n"
    },
    "notes": "Ensure edge case and standard I/O format handling.",
    "editorial": "Print using %e format specifier, which formats numbers like 1.234567e+03."
  },
  "hints": [
    "Scientific notation uses '%e' in C/Java printf.",
    "In Python, f'{val:e}' formats scientific notation.",
    "In JavaScript, pad the exponent digits if needed."
  ],
